1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,240
Knowing that the container owns creation
is useful — until you ask the practical
question: how do my objects actually
receive what they need? Inversion of
Control tells you who is in charge.

2
00:00:10,359 --> 00:00:13,080
Dependency Injection is the everyday
mechanism.

3
00:00:13,199 --> 00:00:25,640
It is how a service gets a repository, how
a controller gets a service, how a gateway
gets a config object — without each class
reaching into a global registry to fetch
friends. Start from a familiar mess.

4
00:00:25,759 --> 00:00:40,320
A class constructs collaborators with new,
or asks a service locator for them, or
reaches into static holders. The class now
knows too much: which implementation
exists, how it is configured, and when it
is born.

5
00:00:40,439 --> 00:00:53,759
Tests inherit that knowledge. Swapping a
real payment client for a fake means
editing production construction paths or
inventing brittle wrappers. Dependency
Injection flips that. The class declares
dependencies.

6
00:00:53,879 --> 00:01:01,799
Someone else supplies them. In Spring,
that someone is the container during
wiring. There are a few injection styles,
and they are not equal in clarity.

7
00:01:01,919 --> 00:01:15,560
Constructor injection passes required
collaborators when the object is created.
Setter injection assigns optional or
mutable dependencies after construction.
Field injection writes directly into
annotated fields.

8
00:01:15,679 --> 00:01:27,639
Spring can do all three. Modern Spring
style strongly prefers constructor
injection for required dependencies,
because the object cannot exist in a half-
built state and the dependencies are
obvious in the signature.

9
00:01:27,759 --> 00:01:39,319
Look at this java for a moment. Walk that
code with the DI lens. @Service is just a
stereotype that helps component scanning
find the class. The constructor lists what
pricing truly needs.

10
00:01:39,439 --> 00:01:49,920
Spring looks at the parameter types, finds
beans that match, and calls the
constructor. Inside quote, there is no
lookup. There is only collaboration.
Notice what became easy.

11
00:01:50,039 --> 00:02:02,399
In a unit test, you can write new
PricingService(new FakeTax(), new
NoDiscount()) without starting Spring at
all. That is not a side benefit. That is
evidence the design is honest.

12
00:02:02,519 --> 00:02:13,919
If you cannot construct the object in a
test without a container, the object is
probably still doing lookup work in
disguise. How does Spring choose which
bean to inject when several candidates
share a type?

13
00:02:14,099 --> 00:02:28,780
It uses type matching first. If more than
one bean fits, you narrow with @Qualifier,
@Primary, or more specific types.
Ambiguity is not a Spring bug — it is the
container refusing to guess when your
graph is unclear.

14
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:38,420
Treat that error as design feedback. Field
injection looks shorter, and that brevity
is exactly why it tempts beginners. Look
at this java for a moment.

15
00:02:38,539 --> 00:02:50,099
It works until testing, immutability, or
required-dependency clarity matters. The
field can remain null if something skips
the container path. The dependencies are
invisible in the constructor.

16
00:02:50,219 --> 00:03:02,340
Prefer constructors for required pieces.
Use setters when a dependency is truly
optional or must change. Keep field
injection for legacy code and narrow
framework cases, not as your default
style.

17
00:03:02,460 --> 00:03:14,900
DI also changes how you think about
interfaces. If PricingService depends on
TaxCalculator, you can ship a local rule
engine today and a remote tax microservice
adapter tomorrow without rewriting
callers.

18
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:21,060
Program to interfaces, inject
implementations. Spring's type-based
wiring makes that natural.

19
00:03:21,180 --> 00:03:33,219
A common misunderstanding is that
Dependency Injection means "put @Autowired
on everything." Autowiring is one way
Spring finds candidates. Injection is the
design principle.

20
00:03:33,340 --> 00:03:44,900
Another misunderstanding is that IoC and
DI are interchangeable words. IoC is the
broader inversion of control. DI is the
specific pattern of supplying dependencies
from the outside.

21
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:48,260
Spring's container is an IoC container
that performs DI.

22
00:03:48,380 --> 00:04:00,540
So today we moved from "the container
creates beans" to "the container supplies
collaborators." We preferred constructor
injection, saw how type matching works,
and named the failure mode of ambiguous
beans.

23
00:04:00,660 --> 00:04:11,980
The next natural question is where those
beans live at the lowest API level — the
factory that can get a bean by name or
type. That is Episode Five — BeanFactory.
